.TH OROC-INSTALL-APP 1 "" "Oro Runtime 0.2.0" "Oro Runtime Manual"
.SH NAME
oroc\-install\-app \- Install the app to the device or host target.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
oroc install\-app [options] [<project\-dir>]
oroc install [options] [<project\-dir>]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Install the app to the device or host target.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-D, \-\-debug\fR
enable debug output
.TP
\fB\-\-device=<identifier>\fR
identifier (ecid, ID) of the device to install to Android defaults to ANDROID_SERIAL or the first ready device
.TP
\fB\-\-platform=<platform>\fR
platform to install application to device (defaults to host):
.br
\(bu android
.br
\(bu ios
.TP
\fB\-P, \-\-prod\fR
install production application
.TP
\fB\-V, \-\-verbose\fR
enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-file=<path>\fR
mirror logs to a JSON file
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SH MACOS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-target=<target>\fR
installation target for macOS application (defaults to '/') the application is installed into '$target/Applications'
.SH NOTES
.PP
Android installs use the first ready device listed by `adb devices` when neither \-\-device nor ANDROID_SERIAL is set. Offline and unauthorized devices are skipped.
.PP
The android\-emulator target selects the first ready emulator.
.SH COMMON ERRORS
.PP
Android: list devices with `adb devices` or pass `\-\-device`. iOS/macOS: list devices with `oroc list\-devices \-\-platform=ios` and pass `\-\-device`.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBoroc install\-app \-\-platform=android \-\-device emulator\-5554\fR
install the current Android build onto a specific emulator
.TP
\fBoroc install\-app \-\-platform=ios \-\-device <udid>\fR
install the current iOS build onto a specific simulator or device
.SH SEE ALSO
oroc(1)
